# 3. FIX 1 — FULL-VIDEO OCR MUST USE TIMELINE-WIDE SAMPLING IN THE ACTUAL PRODUCTION PATH

## Confirmed residual defect

The previous audit identified two sampling paths in `videoProcessor.ts`.

The timeline-wide sampling algorithm exists, but the explicit `sampleIntervalSeconds` path still generates timestamps starting from zero.

The Job Orchestrator still supplies:

- Standard: maximum 15 frames, 4-second interval.
- Deep: maximum 30 frames, 2-second interval.

This can cause long videos to be scanned only during their first 56–58 seconds.

This must be fixed in the actual execution path.

## Required changes

### 3.1 Remove the sampling-path conflict

The Job Orchestrator must not accidentally force the old beginning-only sampling algorithm.

Ensure that the actual `performVideoOcr()` call uses the timeline-wide sampling strategy for both Standard and Deep modes.

You may preserve existing options for compatibility, but they must not override the correct full-duration distribution.

Do not simply remove `sampleIntervalSeconds` without checking other callers and backward compatibility.

### 3.2 Deterministic timeline distribution

Use a deterministic sampling algorithm that distributes target timestamps across the actual video duration.

Requirements:

- Include the beginning.
- Include the middle.
- Include the ending portion.
- Avoid concentrating all samples in the first minute.
- Preserve Standard maximum 15 frames.
- Preserve Deep maximum 30 frames.
- Avoid duplicate timestamps.
- Avoid negative or out-of-range timestamps.
- Handle short videos.
- Handle long videos.
- Handle zero-duration or invalid-duration media.
- Respect existing FFmpeg extraction limits and timeouts.

If scene-aware sampling already exists, preserve it where appropriate.

Do not introduce an external scene-detection service.

### 3.3 Production-path verification

The test must invoke the actual production sampling function used by `performVideoOcr()`.

It must not define a separate test-only `getTimestamps()` implementation that duplicates the expected algorithm.

If necessary, extract the production sampling function into a small reusable module and import that same function from both production code and tests.

Do not create a second implementation for testing.

### 3.4 Required regression cases

Test actual production sampling behavior for:

| Video duration | Mode | Required verification |
|---|---|---|
| 10 minutes | Standard | Samples distributed across the full duration |
| 10 minutes | Deep | Samples distributed across the full duration |
| 30 minutes | Standard | Samples distributed across the full duration |
| 30 minutes | Deep | Samples distributed across the full duration |
| Short video | Both | Valid unique timestamps |
| Zero/invalid duration | Both | Safe and explicit result |

Verify that neither Standard nor Deep falls back to sampling only the first 56–58 seconds.

Add a test specifically reproducing the previous Job Orchestrator options so the original defect cannot return.

